    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d368">

                <pb facs="0693" n="683" sameAs="frus:frus1946v10/pg683"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Marshall Mission Files, Lot 54–D270</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Minutes of Meeting Between General Marshall and Mr.
                        Butterworth</hi><anchor n="75" xml:id="d368-f1"/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">at No. 5 Ning Hai Road, January 6, 1947, 7 p.m.</hi>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <placeName>Nanking</placeName>, <date
                        ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-head" calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1947-01-06T19:00:00+08:00">January 6, 1947, 7 p.m.</date>
                </dateline>
                <table rend="width:50%" rendition="#center">
                    <row>
                        <cell rendition="#nowrap">Also present:</cell>
                        <cell>Colonel Caughey<anchor n="76" xml:id="d368-f2"/>
                        </cell>
                    </row>
                </table>
                <p>[Here follows discussion of various matters of business.]</p>
                <p>General Marshall told Mr. Butterworth that he was quite sure that the Communists
                    would be highly inflamed when they read his forthcoming statement, and their
                    attitude is going to be, no doubt, a strong “To Hell with the United States, and
                    to Hell with the National Government!” General Marshall sought Mr. Butterworth’s
                    views <pb facs="0694" n="684" xml:id="pg684"/> with reference to subsequent
                    procedure as far as the United States was concerned. Mr. Butterworth offered the
                    thought that the United States’ attitude should be a continuation of its
                    relations with the National Government, together with limited assistance.</p>
                <p>General Marshall pointed out that he had spoken to Dr. Wang Wen-hao<anchor n="77"
                        xml:id="d368-f3"/> and stressed to him the necessity of removing the
                    military clique and reactionaries from the governmental structure. General
                    Marshall added that Dr. T. V. Soong<anchor n="78" xml:id="d368-f4"/> is also
                    desirous of removing the military clique and the reactionaries. General Marshall
                    continued by stating that the Generalissimo<anchor n="79" xml:id="d368-f5"/> may
                    also be infuriated, when he reads the statement, to a point that he may be moved
                    toward the necessary governmental reorganization.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
